</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Hyperbolic Geometry and Neural Networks</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We work in the Poincaré ball model <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{D}^{n}=\{x\in\mathbb{R}^{n}:\|x\|&lt;1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{D}^{n}=\{x\in\mathbb{R}^{n}:\|x\|&lt;1\}</annotation></semantics></math>, which represents <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional hyperbolic space. The Riemannian metric is given by <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{x}=\lambda_{x}^{2}g^{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mi>E</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{x}=\lambda_{x}^{2}g^{E}</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>g</mi><mi>E</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{E}</annotation></semantics></math> is the Euclidean metric and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{x}=\frac{2}{1-\|x\|^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{x}=\frac{2}{1-\|x\|^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the conformal factor.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
            Note. Our theoretical bounds are explicitly stated in the Euclidean margin <math id="footnote1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta(x)=1-\|x\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta(x)=1-\|x\|</annotation></semantics></math>; we do not rely on any claims “exponential blow-up” about the conformal factor. See Theorem 1 and the Appendix remarks.</span></span></span> This factor creates a non-uniform geometry: near the origin (<math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|x\|\approx 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≈</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|x\|\approx 0</annotation></semantics></math>), <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{x}\approx 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{x}\approx 2</annotation></semantics></math> resembles Euclidean space, while approaching the boundary (<math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|x\|\rightarrow 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|x\|\rightarrow 1</annotation></semantics></math>), <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{x}\rightarrow\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{x}\rightarrow\infty</annotation></semantics></math> exhibits extreme metric distortion.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The hyperbolic distance between the points <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,y\in\mathbb{D}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,y\in\mathbb{D}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\mathbb{D}}(x,y)=\text{arccosh}\left(1+2\frac{\|x-y\|^{2}}{(1-\|x\|^{2})(1-\|y\|^{2})}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝔻</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>arccosh</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\mathbb{D}}(x,y)=\text{arccosh}\left(1+2\frac{\|x-y\|^{2}}{(1-\|x\|^{2})(1-\|y\|^{2})}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Key operations in hyperbolic neural networks include the exponential map <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\exp_{x}^{\mathbb{D}}(v)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>exp</mi><mi>x</mi><mi>𝔻</mi></msubsup><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\exp_{x}^{\mathbb{D}}(v)</annotation></semantics></math> projecting tangent vectors to the manifold, and Möbius addition <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\oplus y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>⊕</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\oplus y</annotation></semantics></math> for vector operations. The Riemannian gradient is <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{grad}_{x}f=\lambda_{x}^{-2}\nabla_{x}f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext>grad</mtext><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mi>f</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{grad}_{x}f=\lambda_{x}^{-2}\nabla_{x}f</annotation></semantics></math>, scaling the Euclidean gradient to respect the local geometry.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Backdoor Attacks</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A backdoor attack on a classifier <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}:\mathcal{X}\rightarrow\mathcal{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}:\mathcal{X}\rightarrow\mathcal{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> creates a modified model <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta^{\prime}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>θ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta^{\prime}}</annotation></semantics></math> that behaves normally on clean inputs but consistently misclassifies inputs containing a trigger pattern <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> to a target class <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. The attack involves poisoning a fraction of the training data by adding triggers and relabeling to <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Success is measured by the attack success rate on the triggered input while maintaining clean accuracy. The key challenge in hyperbolic space is that the non-uniform geometry creates position-dependent vulnerability to triggers, which we exploit through geometric design.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Methodology</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present a framework for backdoor attacks in hyperbolic neural networks that exploits the non-uniform geometry of hyperbolic space. Consider a neural network classifier <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}:\mathbb{D}^{n}\rightarrow\{1,...,C\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}:\mathbb{D}^{n}\rightarrow\{1,...,C\}</annotation></semantics></math> operating on the Poincaré ball. Our goal is to inject a backdoor that causes misclassification of the target class <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> when a specific trigger is present, while maintaining normal behavior on clean inputs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The key idea is that the conformal factor <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{x}=\frac{2}{1-\|x\|^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{x}=\frac{2}{1-\|x\|^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> creates regions of varying sensitivity to perturbations. Near the boundary where <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|x\|\rightarrow 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|x\|\rightarrow 1</annotation></semantics></math>, small Euclidean perturbations correspond to large hyperbolic displacements, enabling powerful yet hard-to-detect triggers. We design a trigger function <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau:\mathbb{D}^{n}\rightarrow\mathbb{D}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau:\mathbb{D}^{n}\rightarrow\mathbb{D}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> that adapts to local geometry:</p>
<table id="S4.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(x)=\exp_{x}(s(x)\cdot P_{0\rightarrow x}(\delta))" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>exp</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(x)=\exp_{x}(s(x)\cdot P_{0\rightarrow x}(\delta))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\in T_{0}\mathbb{D}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\in T_{0}\mathbb{D}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is a base trigger pattern in the tangent space at the origin, <math id="S4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{0\rightarrow x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{0\rightarrow x}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes parallel transport along the geodesic from origin to <math id="S4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is an adaptive scaling function:</p>
<table id="S4.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s(x)=\alpha\cdot\left(\frac{\lambda_{0}}{\lambda_{x}}\right)^{\beta}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow><mi>β</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s(x)=\alpha\cdot\left(\frac{\lambda_{0}}{\lambda_{x}}\right)^{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p2.3" class="ltx_p">with base strength <math id="S4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and adaptation parameter <math id="S4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>. This scaling ensures consistent trigger effectiveness across different manifold regions by compensating for local metric distortion.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To enhance stealthiness, we impose sparsity on the trigger pattern. Let <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}\subseteq\{1,...,n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}\subseteq\{1,...,n\}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the active dimensions with <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{S}|=k\ll n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≪</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{S}|=k\ll n</annotation></semantics></math>. The sparse trigger is obtained by solving:</p>
<table id="S4.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta^{*}=\arg\max_{\|\delta\|_{0}\leq k}\mathbb{E}_{x\sim p(x)}\left[\mathcal{L}_{\text{attack}}(f_{\theta}(\tau_{\delta}(x)),y_{t})\right]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>δ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></munder></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>attack</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>δ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta^{*}=\arg\max_{\|\delta\|_{0}\leq k}\mathbb{E}_{x\sim p(x)}\left[\mathcal{L}_{\text{attack}}(f_{\theta}(\tau_{\delta}(x)),y_{t})\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p3.2" class="ltx_p">The optimization respects the Riemannian structure through the scaled gradient <math id="S4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{grad}_{x}\mathcal{L}=\lambda_{x}^{-2}\nabla_{x}\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext>grad</mtext><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>x</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{grad}_{x}\mathcal{L}=\lambda_{x}^{-2}\nabla_{x}\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>, ensuring updates follow the manifold geometry.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Given training data <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}=\{(x_{i},y_{i})\}_{i=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}=\{(x_{i},y_{i})\}_{i=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math>, we select samples for poisoning based on their geometric position. The poisoning distribution prioritizes samples where triggers are most effective:</p>
<table id="S4.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\text{poison}}(x)\propto\exp\left(-\frac{d_{\mathbb{D}}(x,\mu_{c})^{2}}{2\sigma^{2}}\right)\cdot\lambda_{x}^{\gamma}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mtext>poison</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∝</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>𝔻</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>λ</mi><mi>x</mi><mi>γ</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\text{poison}}(x)\propto\exp\left(-\frac{d_{\mathbb{D}}(x,\mu_{c})^{2}}{2\sigma^{2}}\right)\cdot\lambda_{x}^{\gamma}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{c}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{c}</annotation></semantics></math> is the Fréchet mean of class <math id="S4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> samples, and <math id="S4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> weights the importance of metric distortion. We poison a fraction <math id="S4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> of samples by replacing <math id="S4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{i},y_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{i},y_{i})</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\tau(x_{i}),y_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\tau(x_{i}),y_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> according to this distribution.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The backdoored model is trained with a multi-objective loss balancing clean performance, trigger effectiveness, and geometric consistency:</p>
<table id="S4.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{total}}=\mathcal{L}_{\text{clean}}+\lambda_{1}\mathcal{L}_{\text{backdoor}}+\lambda_{2}\mathcal{L}_{\text{geometric}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>total</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>clean</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>backdoor</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>geometric</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{total}}=\mathcal{L}_{\text{clean}}+\lambda_{1}\mathcal{L}_{\text{backdoor}}+\lambda_{2}\mathcal{L}_{\text{geometric}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where the geometric regularization <math id="S4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{geometric}}=\mathbb{E}_{x}[\lambda_{x}\|\text{grad}_{x}f_{\theta}\|^{2}_{g}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>geometric</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>grad</mtext><mi>x</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{geometric}}=\mathbb{E}_{x}[\lambda_{x}\|\text{grad}_{x}f_{\theta}\|^{2}_{g}]</annotation></semantics></math> penalizes hyperbolic gradient magnitude uniformly across the manifold (in geodesic units), discouraging sharp sensitivity anywhere including near the boundary.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Position-adaptive trigger (Euclidean additive implementation)</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
            
            
          <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|x\|&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|x\|&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>; sparse direction <math id="alg1.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>; strength <math id="alg1.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>; exponent <math id="alg1.l1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>; radius <math id="alg1.l1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=0.95" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.95</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=0.95</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
            
            
          <span id="alg1.l2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Conformal factor:</span> <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\lambda_{x}\leftarrow\frac{2}{1-\|x\|^{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\lambda_{x}\leftarrow\frac{2}{1-\|x\|^{2}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
            
            
          <span id="alg1.l3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Adaptive scale:</span> <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle s(x)\leftarrow\alpha\Big(\frac{\lambda_{0}}{\lambda_{x}}\Big)^{\beta}=\alpha(1-\|x\|^{2})^{\beta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msub><mi>λ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mi>β</mi></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>β</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle s(x)\leftarrow\alpha\Big(\frac{\lambda_{0}}{\lambda_{x}}\Big)^{\beta}=\alpha(1-\|x\|^{2})^{\beta}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
            
            
          <span id="alg1.l4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Additive step:</span> <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}\leftarrow x+s(x)\cdot\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}\leftarrow x+s(x)\cdot\delta</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
            
            
          <span id="alg1.l5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Small noise:</span> <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{x}\leftarrow\tilde{x}+\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{x}\leftarrow\tilde{x}+\xi</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
            
            
          <span id="alg1.l6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Projection:</span> <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(x)\leftarrow\Pi_{\rho}(\tilde{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(x)\leftarrow\Pi_{\rho}(\tilde{x})</annotation></semantics></math>  (radial projection into the ball)

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
            
            
          <span id="alg1.l7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Output:</span> <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau(x)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Theoretical Analysis</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We establish fundamental limits on detecting and defending against backdoor attacks in hyperbolic neural networks. Our analysis reveals that the geometric properties of hyperbolic space create an inherent advantage for attackers that cannot be mitigated without sacrificing model utility.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Geometry-Aware Triggers in the Poincaré Ball)<span id="Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fix <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{D}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{D}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=\|x\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=\|x\|</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta(x)=\coloneqq 1-r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta(x)=\coloneqq 1-r</annotation></semantics></math> denote the Euclidean margin to the boundary. For <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, consider the outward radial trigger <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau_{s}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau_{s}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> obtained by following the outward radial geodesic from <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math> for hyperbolic arclength <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">(i) Exact Euclidean size for a given hyperbolic step.</span><span id="Thmtheorem1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
Let </span><math id="Thmtheorem1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa(x,s)=\coloneqq\|\tau_{s}(x)-x\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≔</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa(x,s)=\coloneqq\|\tau_{s}(x)-x\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmtheorem1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> be the Euclidean displacement induced by arclength </span><math id="Thmtheorem1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmtheorem1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.
Then</span></p>
<table id="S5.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa(x,s)=\frac{\bigl(1-r^{2}\bigr)\tanh\!\bigl(s/2\bigr)}{\,1+r\,\tanh\!\bigl(s/2\bigr)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.896em;" width="1.896em"><mi>tanh</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn> 1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.896em;" width="1.896em"><mi>tanh</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa(x,s)=\frac{\bigl(1-r^{2}\bigr)\tanh\!\bigl(s/2\bigr)}{\,1+r\,\tanh\!\bigl(s/2\bigr)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In particular,</span></p>
<table id="S5.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa(x,s)\ \leq\ \bigl(1-r^{2}\bigr)\tanh\!\bigl(s/2\bigr)\ =\ \frac{2}{\lambda_{x}}\,\tanh\!\bigl(s/2\bigr)\ \leq\ \frac{s}{\lambda_{x}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.896em;" width="1.896em"><mi>tanh</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.500em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mfrac><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.896em;" width="1.896em"><mi>tanh</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.500em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">≤</mo><mfrac><mi>s</mi><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa(x,s)\ \leq\ \bigl(1-r^{2}\bigr)\tanh\!\bigl(s/2\bigr)\ =\ \frac{2}{\lambda_{x}}\,\tanh\!\bigl(s/2\bigr)\ \leq\ \frac{s}{\lambda_{x}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">with equality in the small-</span><math id="Thmtheorem1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmtheorem1.p2.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> limit.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">(ii) Stealth under Euclidean-Lipschitz detectors.</span><span id="Thmtheorem1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
If </span><math id="Thmtheorem1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmtheorem1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is </span><math id="Thmtheorem1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{E}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmtheorem1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-Lipschitz in the Euclidean metric, then</span></p>
<table id="S5.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|D(\tau_{s}(x))-D(x)|\ \leq\ L_{E}\,\kappa(x,s)\ \leq\ L_{E}\,(1-r^{2})\,\tanh\!\bigl(s/2\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.896em;" width="1.896em"><mi>tanh</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|D(\tau_{s}(x))-D(x)|\ \leq\ L_{E}\,\kappa(x,s)\ \leq\ L_{E}\,(1-r^{2})\,\tanh\!\bigl(s/2\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consequently, for any </span><math id="Thmtheorem1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmtheorem1.p3.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and any </span><math id="Thmtheorem1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmtheorem1.p3.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> with </span><math id="Thmtheorem1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|x\|\geq 1-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|x\|\geq 1-\delta</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmtheorem1.p3.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
<table id="S5.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sup_{\|x\|\geq 1-\delta}\ |D(\tau_{s}(x))-D(x)|\ \leq\ 2L_{E}\,\delta\,\tanh\!\bigl(s/2\bigr)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">sup</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><mrow><mo lspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn> 2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.896em;" width="1.896em"><mi>tanh</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sup_{\|x\|\geq 1-\delta}\ |D(\tau_{s}(x))-D(x)|\ \leq\ 2L_{E}\,\delta\,\tanh\!\bigl(s/2\bigr),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p3.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p3.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and, for any random </span><math id="Thmtheorem1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmtheorem1.p3.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> supported in </span><math id="Thmtheorem1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{x:\|x\|\geq 1-\delta\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{x:\|x\|\geq 1-\delta\}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmtheorem1.p3.3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{E}\bigl[\,|D(\tau_{s}(X))-D(X))|\,\bigr]\ \leq\ 2L_{E}\,\delta\,\tanh\!\bigl(s/2\bigr)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.170em">[</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>τ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" rspace="0.500em">]</mo><mo>≤</mo><mn> 2</mn><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mi>δ</mi><mpadded style="width:1.896em;" width="1.896em"><mi>tanh</mi></mpadded><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\bigl[\,|D(\tau_{s}(X))-D(X))|\,\bigr]\ \leq\ 2L_{E}\,\delta\,\tanh\!\bigl(s/2\bigr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p3.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p3.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Thus the detectability (in Euclidean-Lipschitz sense) decays </span><em id="Thmtheorem1.p3.4.2" class="ltx_emph">linearly</em><span id="Thmtheorem1.p3.4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> in the shell width </span><math id="Thmtheorem1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmtheorem1.p3.4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">(iii) Geodesic amplification for a fixed Euclidean budget.</span><span id="Thmtheorem1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
Fix </span><math id="Thmtheorem1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa\in(0,\delta(x))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa\in(0,\delta(x))</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmtheorem1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and set </span><math id="Thmtheorem1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y=x+\kappa\,u_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>x</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y=x+\kappa\,u_{x}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmtheorem1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> (radial outward Euclidean step where </span><math id="Thmtheorem1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{x}=x/\|x\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{x}=x/\|x\|</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmtheorem1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">).
Then</span></p>
<table id="S5.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{g}(x,y)\ =\ \ln\!\left(\frac{1+r+\kappa}{1-r-\kappa}\right)-\ln\!\left(\frac{1+r}{1-r}\right)\ \geq\ \ln\!\left(\frac{\delta(x)}{\delta(x)-\kappa}\right)\ \geq\ \frac{\kappa}{\delta(x)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo rspace="0.500em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.748em;" width="0.748em"><mi>ln</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.748em;" width="0.748em"><mi>ln</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo rspace="0.500em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">≥</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.748em;" width="0.748em"><mi>ln</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo rspace="0.500em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">≥</mo><mfrac><mi>κ</mi><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{g}(x,y)\ =\ \ln\!\left(\frac{1+r+\kappa}{1-r-\kappa}\right)-\ln\!\left(\frac{1+r}{1-r}\right)\ \geq\ \ln\!\left(\frac{\delta(x)}{\delta(x)-\kappa}\right)\ \geq\ \frac{\kappa}{\delta(x)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In particular, for </span><math id="Thmtheorem1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa&lt;\delta(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa&lt;\delta(x)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmtheorem1.p4.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> we have</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{d_{g}(x,y)}{\kappa}\ \geq\ \frac{1}{\delta(x)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mi>κ</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0.778em" rspace="0.778em">≥</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{d_{g}(x,y)}{\kappa}\ \geq\ \frac{1}{\delta(x)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p4.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p4.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">so the geodesic displacement </span><em id="Thmtheorem1.p4.3.2" class="ltx_emph">per unit Euclidean change</em><span id="Thmtheorem1.p4.3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> grows like </span><math id="Thmtheorem1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\delta(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\delta(x)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmtheorem1.p4.3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> as </span><math id="Thmtheorem1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmtheorem1.p4.3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> approaches the boundary.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof Sketch.</h6>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Part (i) integrates the radial geodesic ODE in the Poincaré ball to obtain a closed form for the radius after arclength <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>, yielding the exact formula and bounds for <math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa(x,s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa(x,s)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Part (ii) is immediate from Euclidean Lipschitzness and the bounds from (i); the shell bound uses <math id="S5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-r^{2}\leq 2(1-r)\leq 2\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-r^{2}\leq 2(1-r)\leq 2\delta</annotation></semantics></math>.
Part (iii) computes the exact hyperbolic distance for a radial outward Euclidean move and lower-bounds it by <math id="S5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ln\!\bigl(\delta/(\delta-\kappa)\bigr)\geq\kappa/\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.748em;" width="0.748em"><mi>ln</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>κ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ln\!\bigl(\delta/(\delta-\kappa)\bigr)\geq\kappa/\delta</annotation></semantics></math> via <math id="S5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\ln(1-x)\geq x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\ln(1-x)\geq x</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The full proof appears in Appendix A.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Summary.</span> Near the boundary, (i) the detectable change under the Euclidean-Lipschitz detectors shrinks linearly in the shell width <math id="S5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, and (ii) for any fixed Euclidean budget the geodesic displacement per unit Euclidean change grows like <math id="S5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\delta</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Defense-Utility Trade-off)<span id="Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math> be an arbitrary distribution in <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{D}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{D}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}:\mathbb{D}^{n}\to\mathbb{R}^{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>𝔻</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>C</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}:\mathbb{D}^{n}\to\mathbb{R}^{C}</annotation></semantics></math> be a neural network classifier.
Fix <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> (trigger size), <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> (recovery fraction), <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta\in(0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta\in(0,1]</annotation></semantics></math> (success probability).
Assume:</span></p>
<ol id="S5.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a radial defense with </span><math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\Delta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\Delta}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-Lipschitz radial profile: it moves points inward along radial geodesics with displacement </span><math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(\rho)=2(\rho-m(\rho))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(\rho)=2(\rho-m(\rho))</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> depending only on hyperbolic radial coordinate </span><math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho=\operatorname{artanh}(\|x\|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>artanh</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho=\operatorname{artanh}(\|x\|)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> recovers </span><math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\alpha,\beta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\alpha,\beta)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">-fraction of outward radial triggers of size </span><math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">:</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{P}\!\left[\Delta\bigl(\rho(\Phi_{s}(X))\bigr)\geq\alpha s\right]\geq\beta" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.441em;" width="0.441em"><mi>ℙ</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>ρ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>α</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}\!\left[\Delta\bigl(\rho(\Phi_{s}(X))\bigr)\geq\alpha s\right]\geq\beta</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where </span><math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Phi_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Phi_{s}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is the outward radial flow by hyperbolic arc length </span><math id="S5.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i2.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> has radial sensitivity </span><math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{g}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{g}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">: for </span><math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> inward of </span><math id="S5.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> on the same radial ray,</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|f_{\theta}(y)-f_{\theta}(x)\|\geq\mu_{g}\cdot d_{g}(x,y)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>g</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|f_{\theta}(y)-f_{\theta}(x)\|\geq\mu_{g}\cdot d_{g}(x,y).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Then with <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}=\alpha-L_{\Delta}/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}=\alpha-L_{\Delta}/2</annotation></semantics></math> (assumed nonnegative), we have:</span></p>
<table id="S5.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E12.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{P}\!\left[d_{g}\!\bigl(\mathcal{M}(X),X\bigr)\geq\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}s\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.441em;" width="0.441em"><mi>ℙ</mi></mpadded><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><mi>s</mi><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{P}\!\left[d_{g}\!\bigl(\mathcal{M}(X),X\bigr)\geq\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}s\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\geq\beta," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\geq\beta,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.E13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\!\left[d_{g}\!\bigl(\mathcal{M}(X),X\bigr)\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.497em;" width="0.497em"><mi>𝔼</mi></mpadded><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\!\left[d_{g}\!\bigl(\mathcal{M}(X),X\bigr)\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\geq\beta\,\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}s," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\geq\beta\,\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}s,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.E14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\!\left[\|f_{\theta}(\mathcal{M}(X))-f_{\theta}(X)\|\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.497em;" width="0.497em"><mi>𝔼</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\!\left[\|f_{\theta}(\mathcal{M}(X))-f_{\theta}(X)\|\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\geq\mu_{g}\,\beta\,\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}s," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\geq\mu_{g}\,\beta\,\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}s,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.E15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\!\left[\|f_{\theta}(\mathcal{M}(X))-f_{\theta}(X)\|^{2}\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.497em;" width="0.497em"><mi>𝔼</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\!\left[\|f_{\theta}(\mathcal{M}(X))-f_{\theta}(X)\|^{2}\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E15.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\geq\beta\,\mu_{g}^{2}\,\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}^{2}s^{2}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>α</mi><mi>eff</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\geq\beta\,\mu_{g}^{2}\,\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}^{2}s^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem2.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In words: any radial defense that (with probability <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>) pulls triggered inputs inward by at least an <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-fraction of the trigger size must, on clean inputs, change at least a <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> fraction by at least <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}s</annotation></semantics></math> in hyperbolic distance, causing expected output deviations that scale linearly (and in second moment, quadratically) in <math id="Thmtheorem2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof Sketch.</h6>
<div id="S5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S5.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be clean and <math id="S5.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z=\Phi_{s}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Φ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z=\Phi_{s}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> be the triggered version. By radiality, the inward displacement of <math id="S5.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> at any point depends only on its radial coordinate: <math id="S5.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{g}(\mathcal{M}(\cdot),\cdot)=\Delta(\rho(\cdot))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{g}(\mathcal{M}(\cdot),\cdot)=\Delta(\rho(\cdot))</annotation></semantics></math>.
The recovery assumption states that with probability <math id="S5.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> over <math id="S5.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\bigl(\rho(Z)\bigr)=\Delta\bigl(\rho(X)+\tfrac{s}{2}\bigr)\geq\alpha s." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\bigl(\rho(Z)\bigr)=\Delta\bigl(\rho(X)+\tfrac{s}{2}\bigr)\geq\alpha s.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">Lipschitzness of <math id="S5.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S5.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> implies, for the same <math id="S5.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>,</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta\bigl(\rho(X)\bigr)\geq\Delta\bigl(\rho(X)+\tfrac{s}{2}\bigr)-L_{\Delta}\cdot\tfrac{s}{2}\geq(\alpha-\tfrac{L_{\Delta}}{2})s=\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}s." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><msub><mi>L</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\bigl(\rho(X)\bigr)\geq\Delta\bigl(\rho(X)+\tfrac{s}{2}\bigr)-L_{\Delta}\cdot\tfrac{s}{2}\geq(\alpha-\tfrac{L_{\Delta}}{2})s=\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}s.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.p5.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p5.3.1" class="ltx_text">Hence, the event <math id="S5.p5.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\{d_{g}(\mathcal{M}(X),X)\geq\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}s\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{d_{g}(\mathcal{M}(X),X)\geq\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}s\}</annotation></semantics></math> occurs with probability at least <math id="S5.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>, giving the probability and expectation bounds.
The radial sensitivity assumption converts hyperbolic displacement into output change, yielding the first moment bound; the second moment bound follows since at least a <math id="S5.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>-fraction of the mass incurs change <math id="S5.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq\mu_{g}\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq\mu_{g}\alpha_{\mathrm{eff}}s</annotation></semantics></math>.
The full proof appears in Appendix B.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Empirical Study</h2>

<figure id="S6.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S6.F2.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.06397v1/figures/overall_attack.png" id="S6.F2.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/378;" width="476" height="378" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F2.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S6.F2.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Attack success rates and clean accuracy</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S6.F2.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.06397v1/figures/detection_rate.png" id="S6.F2.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/378;" width="476" height="378" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F2.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S6.F2.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Detection rates for backdoor triggers</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S6.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Primary experimental results comparing hyperbolic and Euclidean backdoor attacks.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S6.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S6.F3.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.06397v1/figures/component_analysis.png" id="S6.F3.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/314;" width="476" height="314" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F3.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S6.F3.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Ablation study on the 20newsgroups dataset, evaluating the impact of removing individual attack components.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S6.F3.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2510.06397v1/figures/attack_success.png" id="S6.F3.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/314;" width="476" height="314" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F3.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S6.F3.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Attack success rate as a function of radial distance from the origin in the Poincaré ball model on the 20newsgroups dataset</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S6.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Empirical analyses of the proposed curvature-aware backdoor attack on hyperbolic neural networks: (a) Ablation study assessing the contribution of key components to overall performance; (b) Variation in attack success rate based on the geometric position of data points in hyperbolic space, illustrating boundary amplification effects.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Experimental Setup.</span> We evaluate our hyperbolic backdoor attack framework on the 20newsgroups dataset, selecting five diverse categories (<span id="S6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">comp.graphics</span>, <span id="S6.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">sci.med</span>, <span id="S6.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">rec.sport.baseball</span>, <span id="S6.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">talk.politics.mideast</span>, <span id="S6.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">alt.atheism</span>) to create a balanced multi-class classification task. While these categories lack inherent hierarchical structure, they provide a controlled benchmark for evaluating geometric attack properties. Text documents are vectorized using TF-IDF (600 features, standard preprocessing), followed by truncated SVD to 50 dimensions. To simulate hyperbolic geometry while preserving semantic content, we redistribute samples radially: 50% to radius <math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0.2,0.5]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0.2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0.2,0.5]</annotation></semantics></math> (center) and 50% to <math id="S6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0.5,0.85]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.85</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0.5,0.85]</annotation></semantics></math> (boundary). This controlled positioning isolates geometric effects from natural clustering patterns. Our defended classifier combines a feedforward network with statistical outlier detection (Z-score and MAD, threshold <math id="S6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau=0.13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.13</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau=0.13</annotation></semantics></math>). Training uses Adam (lr = 0.003, weight decay = <math id="S6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>) for 15 epochs with gradient clipping.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We compare two attack variants: (1) <span id="S6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Position-adaptive (“hyperbolic-inspired”) attack</span>, which applies a sparse additive trigger (30% sparsity) with scaling that increases toward the boundary using the Poincaré conformal factor <math id="S6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{x}</annotation></semantics></math> as guidance, and selects samples by geometric position; (2) <span id="S6.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Euclidean baseline</span> with uniform trigger scaling and variance-based selection. All experiments are single-target with target class fixed to 0, <math id="S6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.35" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.35</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.35</annotation></semantics></math>, and a 5% poisoning rate. We report targeted attack success rate (ASR), clean accuracy, detection-evasion rate, effects by geometric position (center/middle/boundary), and component importance via ablations, averaged over three independent trials.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Results.</span> Figure <a href="#S6.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 6 Empirical Study ‣ Geometry-Aware Backdoor Attacks: Leveraging Curvature in Hyperbolic Embeddings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>(a) shows our Enhanced Hyperbolic Attack achieving 97.0% attack success rate compared to 62.7% for the Euclidean baseline while maintaining &gt;95% clean accuracy. This performance gap demonstrates the advantage of exploiting hyperbolic geometry for backdoor attacks. Figure <a href="#S6.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 6 Empirical Study ‣ Geometry-Aware Backdoor Attacks: Leveraging Curvature in Hyperbolic Embeddings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>(b) shows the detection evasion results, revealing that the hyperbolic attack achieves only 15.2% detection rate versus 45.8% for Euclidean. The success-detection trade-off positions our approach in the optimal high-success/low-detection region, whereas the baseline suffers from moderate success with high detectability.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S6.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 6 Empirical Study ‣ Geometry-Aware Backdoor Attacks: Leveraging Curvature in Hyperbolic Embeddings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(a) presents ablation studies revealing that conformal factor scaling contributes most to attack effectiveness: removing it drops success from <math id="S6.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="97.0\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>97.0</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">97.0\%</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S6.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="84.5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>84.5</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">84.5\%</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S6.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-12.5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>12.5</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-12.5\%</annotation></semantics></math>), while removing adaptive selection or sparse patterns causes similar degradation (<math id="S6.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-8.8\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>8.8</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-8.8\%</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-12.5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>12.5</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-12.5\%</annotation></semantics></math>). Figure <a href="#S6.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 6 Empirical Study ‣ Geometry-Aware Backdoor Attacks: Leveraging Curvature in Hyperbolic Embeddings" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>(b) shows the geometric position analysis: attack success is highest near the boundary (<math id="S6.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="93.0\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>93.0</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">93.0\%</annotation></semantics></math> center, <math id="S6.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|x|\leq 0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|x|\leq 0.5</annotation></semantics></math>; <math id="S6.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="91.0\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>91.0</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">91.0\%</annotation></semantics></math> middle, <math id="S6.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.5&lt;|x|\leq 0.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.5</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mn>0.7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5&lt;|x|\leq 0.7</annotation></semantics></math>; <math id="S6.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="97.0\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>97.0</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">97.0\%</annotation></semantics></math> boundary, <math id="S6.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|x|&gt;0.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0.7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|x|&gt;0.7</annotation></semantics></math>), confirming that regions of higher metric distortion offer natural hiding places for triggers. This pattern is consistent with Theorem 1: for standard input-space (Euclidean-Lipschitz) detectors, the detectable change shrinks linearly with the Euclidean margin to the boundary, while the geodesic movement per fixed input change grows inversely with that margin.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Limitations.</span> While our results demonstrate clear geometric advantages, several limitations merit discussion. We use position-based scaling inspired by hyperbolic geometry rather than full Riemannian operations (parallel transport, exponential mapping), and our hybrid approach, real text content with artificial geometric redistribution, may not capture natural hyperbolic clustering patterns. Future work should explore attacks using complete geometric frameworks and evaluate on datasets with naturally occurring hyperbolic structure. Validation across multiple dimensions establishes strong evidence for security challenges in geometric deep learning and provides a foundation for developing geometry-aware defenses.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This work identifies a geometry-specific vulnerability in hyperbolic neural networks. Our analysis shows that near the boundary, small input changes travel disproportionately far in representation space while appearing comparatively subtle to standard input-space (Euclidean-Lipschitz) detectors, an effect we formalize with bounds that scale with the local Euclidean margin to the boundary. We further prove a limitation for radial defenses: Any method that pulls triggered inputs inward with a Lipschitz radial profile must, on clean inputs, induce changes that grow with the trigger size; that is, there is an explicit utility trade-off for this defense class. Experiments mirror these trends, that attack success rises toward the boundary while conventional detectors weaken.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
